# Copy to secrets.env (gitignored) and paste the token secret.
#
# Create the token in the Proxmox UI:
#   Datacenter -> Permissions -> API Tokens -> Add
#     User:     root@pam
#     Token ID: claude
#
# IMPORTANT: "Privilege Separation" is ticked by default, and a separated token
# starts with NO permissions at all - every call returns 403 until you grant it
# a role. Either untick it, or leave it ticked and add:
#   Datacenter -> Permissions -> Add -> API Token Permission
#     Path: /        Token: root@pam!claude        Role: PVEAuditor
#
# PVEAuditor is read-only: it can see every node, VM, config and metric, and
# change nothing. That is all this module needs.
#
# The secret is displayed exactly once, when the token is created.
PVE_URL=https://<proxmox-ip>:8006
PVE_TOKEN_ID=root@pam!claude
PVE_TOKEN_SECRET=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The write path: root on the hypervisor, over SSH.
#
# This is a general administrative key for the Proxmox host, not a per-project
# one. It is root, so it can do anything pct, qm and pvesh can do -- create and
# destroy guests, edit their configs, read every disk, reboot the box. Whatever
# gets automated against this host next uses this same key; there is no second,
# narrower one to find. It mirrors ~/.ssh/ha_ed25519 for the HA guest, same
# convention and same comment.
#
# What the read/write split does and does not buy you: PVE_TOKEN_* above is
# PVEAuditor, so anything reaching the hypervisor for metrics CANNOT alter it,
# even by accident or by a bug. That is worth having. It does not constrain
# this key at all -- an SSH root shell is an SSH root shell. The split makes
# intent legible, not capability limited, and the caution belongs here rather
# than in the scripts.
#
# Generate the key on the workstation:
#
#   ssh-keygen -t ed25519 -f ~/.ssh/pve_ed25519 -N "" -C "you@workstation"
#
# Install the public half ONCE, from the Proxmox UI (Datacenter -> proxmox ->
# Shell), where you are already root and need no key to get in:
#
#   mkdir -p /root/.ssh && chmod 700 /root/.ssh
#   echo 'ssh-ed25519 AAAA... you@workstation' >> /root/.ssh/authorized_keys
#   chmod 600 /root/.ssh/authorized_keys
#
# Verify:  sh Proxmox/tools/pve_ssh.sh pveversion
#
# Revoke by deleting that line from /root/.ssh/authorized_keys. Unlike the API
# token there is no UI panel for it, which is the one practical cost of using
# SSH here -- so it is worth knowing where the line lives before you need it
# gone.
#
# PVE_SSH_KEY defaults to ~/.ssh/pve_ed25519 and PVE_SSH_USER to root, so
# PVE_SSH_HOST is the only line that has to be here.
PVE_SSH_HOST=<proxmox-ip>
#PVE_SSH_USER=root
#PVE_SSH_KEY=/home/you/.ssh/pve_ed25519
